Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they will be used  
• `stn`: station identifier – filter for the single station ‘723758’.  
• `year`: filter to 2009.  
• `mo`: filter to 10 (October).  
• `da`: day-of‑month; combine with `year` and `mo` to build a full date string.  
• `temp`: mean daily temperature – use it to rank the warmest October days.  
Optional filter conditions  
• `temp <> 9999.9`  (exclude missing values).  
• `count_temp > 0`  (only keep rows that actually had observations).

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final BigQuery SQL statements (all read‑only “SELECT …” queries; each includes a short comment and LIMIT 20).  
They progress from very simple inspections up to the final query that answers the task.

```sql
-- 1. Quick peek at the 2009 table
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Distinct years present (sanity‑check)
SELECT DISTINCT year
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Distinct months reported by the target station in 2009
SELECT DISTINCT mo
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND year = '2009'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. All October rows for that station
SELECT year, mo, da, temp, count_temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND year = '2009'
  AND mo   = '10'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Same rows but excluding missing temperatures
SELECT year, mo, da, temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND year = '2009'
  AND mo   = '10'
  AND temp <> 9999.9
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Build a proper date string for October 2009 rows
SELECT CONCAT(year,'-',mo,'-',da) AS yyyymmdd, temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND year = '2009'
  AND mo   = '10'
  AND temp <> 9999.9
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Rank October 2009 days by mean temperature (descending)
SELECT
  CONCAT(year,'-',mo,'-',da) AS yyyymmdd,
  temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND year = '2009'
  AND mo   = '10'
  AND temp <> 9999.9
ORDER BY temp DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Add an integer rank column to see ordering clearly
SELECT
  CONCAT(year,'-',mo,'-',da)             AS yyyymmdd,
  temp,
  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY temp DESC) AS temp_rank
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND year = '2009'
  AND mo   = '10'
  AND temp <> 9999.9
ORDER BY temp DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Join with the station‑metadata table (optional context)
SELECT
  s.name,
  g.year, g.mo, g.da, g.temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009` AS g
LEFT JOIN `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.stations` AS s
       ON g.stn = s.usaf AND g.wban = s.wban
WHERE g.stn = '723758'
  AND g.year = '2009'
  AND g.mo   = '10'
  AND g.temp <> 9999.9
ORDER BY g.temp DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: top‑3 warmest October 2009 days (YYYY‑MM‑DD)
SELECT
  CONCAT(g.year,'-',LPAD(g.mo,2,'0'),'-',LPAD(g.da,2,'0')) AS date,
  g.temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009` AS g
WHERE g.stn  = '723758'
  AND g.year = '2009'
  AND g.mo   = '10'
  AND g.temp <> 9999.9
ORDER BY g.temp DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

Running the last query will return the three October 2009 dates with the highest mean temperature for station **723758**, in the requested `YYYY-MM-DD` format.
